    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-8">US ESIGN Act & UETA</h1>

    <h2>The Electronic Signatures in Global and National Commerce Act (ESIGN)</h2>
    <p>Passed in 2000, the ESIGN Act is a federal law that grants electronic signatures and records the same legal status as traditional handwritten signatures and paper documents.</p>

    <h2>Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (UETA)</h2>
    <p>UETA was adopted by 49 states (New York has a similar law) to establish a uniform framework for electronic transactions at the state level.</p>

    <h3>Key Requirements for Legality:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Intent to sign:</strong> The signer must demonstrate clear intent to sign electronically (e.g., clicking an "I Agree" or "Sign" button).</li>
        <li><strong>Consent to do business electronically:</strong> The parties must agree to conduct the transaction electronically.</li>
        <li><strong>Association of signature with the record:</strong> The system used to capture the transaction must keep an associated record that reflects the process by which the signature was created, or generate a graphic statement (which usignpdf provides via our <a href="/glossary#audit-trail">Audit Trail</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Record retention:</strong> The system must securely store the document for future reference.</li>
    </ol>
